-There is **no linter run** (`eslint.ignoreDuringBuilds` is on), and `npm test` covers a deliberately short list: `overlaps()` and `selectPromotable()` in `orchestrator.ts`, `normalizePolicy` / `evaluateBudget` in `budget.ts`, `isUsageLimit` / `isTransientApiError` / `refusalResumeAt` / `nextPrompt` in `orchestrator.ts`, where `buildSessionBlocks` puts a boundary in `windows.ts` — both derived and under `sessionResetOverrideAt`, the NUL-record parsers and the patch budget in `diff.ts`, `parseMergeTree` / `landRefusal` / `hasConflictMarkers` / `conflictRegions` / `parseStatusZ` / `commitRefusal` / `purgeRefusal` in `land.ts`, `planItem` in `mergeQueue.ts`, `normalizeTemplateInput` / `rowToTemplate` in `templates.ts`, `githubEnv` / `buildArgs` / `permissionDenials` / `needsLiveSpendTelemetry` in `orchestrator.ts`, `planProposal` / `composeTask` / `chatPrompt` in `chat.ts` with `githubSlug` in `workspace.ts`, and `cycleOutputs` in `cycles.ts`. Every one earns it on the same grounds — pure functions whose failure modes are silent and expensive (two agents in one working tree, or a run queued behind something that will never move; a run that never terminates, or that parks instead of ending; a wall that stops being recognised, or a run that waits hours for money that will not arrive; a blip that ends a run holding a live session, or three billed re-spawns into a wall that will refuse every one; a cycle billed to say `DONE` twice, or one that restates a task already half done; a window that rolls over while the provider is still counting it, or that reopens after it closed; a file list that shifts by one at the first rename, or a diff that is quietly a third of the change; a merge into a dirty tree or onto a branch the work does not belong on; a conflicting file dropped from the list because a message did not parse, or a markdown heading rendered as a clash; a billed resolution bought for a merge that was going to be refused anyway, or ten identical refusals where one was the answer; a saved configuration that widens what an agent may do, or that can never be started; a credential block git discards whole because its count disagrees with its pairs; a guard read off a model's suggestion instead of off the guard set a person wrote, or an agent handed a heading with no instructions above it, or an agent ordered to commit by a flag that forbids committing, or a refusal that leaves a run looking like it chose to do nothing, or a chat turn billed without the thread it is answering, or issues read out of a stranger's repository because a remote URL was parsed loosely, or a mid-cycle spending limit reading a figure that cannot move because the run was never told to report one; or one run's leftovers committed onto the branch of whichever run took its checkout slot next, or a branch destroyed by a request that was aimed at a different one, or a work cycle's report filed under the cycle before it). Three are renderings rather than functions — `Meter.test.tsx`, `LiveTelemetry.test.tsx` and `Markdown.test.tsx` — and they clear the same bar by the same argument: what they pin is *something the reader would act on*, wrong in a way that throws nothing and typechecks (a solid green bar that means "we don't know"; a first-party figure that reads as an addend to the transcript-derived meter beside it; a report that ends in silence because the cycle was killed with a code fence still open). That is the bar for adding another, not a general testing convention to follow. `npm run typecheck` plus a `docker compose up --build` smoke test is still the real verification loop, and the README's "Verified" section records what was checked by hand — including a "Not yet verified" list, which must stay honest.
+There is **no linter run** (`eslint.ignoreDuringBuilds` is on), and `npm test` covers a deliberately short list: `overlaps()` and `selectPromotable()` in `orchestrator.ts`, `normalizePolicy` / `evaluateBudget` in `budget.ts`, `isUsageLimit` / `isTransientApiError` / `refusalResumeAt` / `nextPrompt` in `orchestrator.ts`, where `buildSessionBlocks` puts a boundary in `windows.ts` — both derived and under `sessionResetOverrideAt`, the NUL-record parsers and the patch budget in `diff.ts`, `parseMergeTree` / `landRefusal` / `hasConflictMarkers` / `conflictRegions` / `parseStatusZ` / `commitRefusal` / `purgeRefusal` in `land.ts`, `planItem` in `mergeQueue.ts`, `normalizeTemplateInput` / `rowToTemplate` in `templates.ts`, `githubEnv` / `buildArgs` / `permissionDenials` / `needsLiveSpendTelemetry` in `orchestrator.ts`, `planProposal` / `composeTask` / `chatPrompt` in `chat.ts` with `githubSlug` in `workspace.ts`, and `cycleOutputs` in `cycles.ts`. Every one earns it on the same grounds — pure functions whose failure modes are silent and expensive (two agents in one working tree, or a run queued behind something that will never move; a run that never terminates, or that parks instead of ending; a wall that stops being recognised, or a run that waits hours for money that will not arrive; a blip that ends a run holding a live session, or three billed re-spawns into a wall that will refuse every one; a cycle billed to say `DONE` twice, or one that restates a task already half done; a window that rolls over while the provider is still counting it, or that reopens after it closed; a file list that shifts by one at the first rename, or a diff that is quietly a third of the change; a merge into a dirty tree or onto a branch the work does not belong on; a conflicting file dropped from the list because a message did not parse, or a markdown heading rendered as a clash; a billed resolution bought for a merge that was going to be refused anyway, or ten identical refusals where one was the answer; a saved configuration that widens what an agent may do, or that can never be started; a credential block git discards whole because its count disagrees with its pairs; a guard read off a model's suggestion instead of off the guard set a person wrote, or an agent handed a heading with no instructions above it, or an agent ordered to commit by a flag that forbids committing, or a refusal that leaves a run looking like it chose to do nothing, or a chat turn billed without the thread it is answering, or issues read out of a stranger's repository because a remote URL was parsed loosely, or a mid-cycle spending limit reading a figure that cannot move because the run was never told to report one; or one run's leftovers committed onto the branch of whichever run took its checkout slot next, or a branch destroyed by a request that was aimed at a different one, or a work cycle's report filed under the cycle before it). Three are renderings rather than functions — `Meter.test.tsx`, `LiveTelemetry.test.tsx` and `Markdown.test.tsx` — and they clear the same bar by the same argument: what they pin is *something the reader would act on*, wrong in a way that throws nothing and typechecks (a solid green bar that means "we don't know"; a first-party figure that reads as an addend to the transcript-derived meter beside it; a report that ends in silence because the cycle was killed with a code fence still open). One — `chatTurn.test.ts` — is neither, and is the only test here that opens the database: it drives `sendChatMessage` through a failed pre-spawn setup, because what it pins is a *state transition* rather than a return value (a chat thread stranded at `thinking` for ever by one failed `writeFileSync`, unusable until the server restarts). It sets `DATA_DIR`/`CLAUDE_HOME` into a temp directory before the module graph loads, which is why it is a second file rather than a case in `chat.test.ts`, and it refuses to run if `config.ts` was already loaded. That is the bar for adding another, not a general testing convention to follow. `npm run typecheck` plus a `docker compose up --build` smoke test is still the real verification loop, and the README's "Verified" section records what was checked by hand — including a "Not yet verified" list, which must stay honest.

 Note that `npm run dev` on the host reads the host's **real** `~/.claude` transcripts and can spawn **real, billed** `claude` processes. Runs default to `acceptEdits`, so an agent started from the UI writes files.

